From 262accc8ac53c34cd649d4df49c0cf22999eb10d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Naster17 <naster17.dev@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 17:26:30 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 23/23] Fuse the gated-delta solve and chunk scan behind an
 opt-in flag

The native chunk op still spends most of its time in two batched ATen
triangular solves and the sequential Python chunk scan (D68 profile: 70%
scan at B1/H16/D128/L1024, 48% solves at the tiny single-chunk geometry).
Add use_fused_solve_scan=True: one in-register Triton unit-triangular
inverse with analytic backward replaces both solves, and a column-blocked
linear-recurrence scan (S_{i+1} = M_i S_i + N_i) with an adjoint backward
replaces the loop; the M/N/P/R precompute stays autograd-visible ATen.
The fused path applies from two chunks up (measured crossover: every
variant regresses at one chunk) and refuses above a 256 MiB saved-state
cap. The default branch keeps the exact operation order.

Test Plan: extended test_bc250_gated_delta.py (fused CPU-FP64 recurrence,
fused-vs-default equivalence, low-precision reference preparation,
refusals) passes 72/72 on native gfx1013 under the immutable release;
numpy float64 math/gradient checks and the D68 qualification fixture
(op parity, CPU anchor, timing, model parity/timing) are retained in
TheRock build_tools. Full upstream CI was not run.
